{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q004_ferritin_like_functional_divergence","answer":"Verified SCOPe facts (release 2.08, stable): all three entities sit in one Ferritin-like superfamily, a.25.1 (sunid 47240), inside the Ferritin-like fold a.25 (sunid 47239) and the All alpha proteins class a (sunid 46456). Within a.25.1 the families are a.25.1.1 Ferritin (sunid 47241), a.25.1.2 Ribonucleotide reductase-like (sunid 47253), and a.25.1.3 Manganese catalase (T-catalase) (sunid 100951). The protein-level nodes retrieved from search and lineage calls are (Apo)ferritin 47246 (a.25.1.1), Ribonucleotide reductase R2 47257 (a.25.1.2), and Manganese catalase (T-catalase) 47263 (a.25.1.3). The superfamily annotation says only 'contains bimetal-ion centre in the middle of the bundle'; it does not name the metals. Homology flags: the superfamily, ferritin, RNR-like, and Mn-catalase family nodes all report homology_implied_by_level true with no warnings, and the RNR-like family annotations are empty.

Interpretation: SCOPe places ferritins, RNR R2-like proteins, and Mn catalases in one superfamily, so it asserts probable common ancestry at the superfamily level, as the hierarchy's homology-bearing level. Because the three sit in different families (a.25.1.1, a.25.1.2, a.25.1.3), SCOPe does not place them as close relatives. The shared fold and the bimetal-centre annotation support a common four-helix-bundle architecture, not a shared reaction. The chemical differences you mention (iron storage and ferroxidation for ferritin; a diiron-tyrosyl radical cofactor for RNR R2; dimanganese catalysis of H2O2 dismutation for Mn catalase) come from my pretrained knowledge. They are not encoded in or verified by these API responses, and I did not retrieve sequence, alignment, or structure data for them.

Limits: a superfamily-level relationship says nothing about specific substrate, metal identity, reaction, or oligomeric state, and I do not infer those from placement. Divergent chemistry within one superfamily is compatible with SCOPe's assertion and does not contradict it. Distinct chemistries also do not establish nonhomology or convergence; SCOPe makes no such claim. No sequence identity, alignment, or domain-level boundary was checked, and I did not query other releases, so no historical-change claim is made. Retrieved classifications are protein nodes, not chain-level domain assignments.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","scop-homology/references/api-usage.md","scop-homology/references/biological-interpretation.md","/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/releases","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=ferritin&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=ribonucleotide+reductase&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=catalase&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=manganese+catalase&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=Mn+catalase&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47246/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47257/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47263/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/100951/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47240/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47240/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47241/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47253/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47253/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/100951/homology"]}]}
